{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:growth:redteam","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:0d750b266637f0bde9877d7963e840a54a087201402e91c8b4e608ed98972b8f","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.36},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Conjunct A (GDP-weighted OECD-38 core CPI/PCE >=2.50%/yr, 2027-2036) = 0.79. P3 found the single best fact in all of Wave 1 and P1/P2 ignored it, scoring 0.44 and 0.52 off a Germany/US/Japan mental model. A PPP-GDP-weighted OECD-38 core aggregate must include Turkey (~3.5-4% weight, core not below 8% in a decade), Mexico (~3.5%, core ~4%), Poland (~2%), Colombia/Chile/Costa Rica/Hungary (~2.5% combined). Arithmetic: Turkey at 15% core contributes ~+0.55 pp, Mexico ~+0.15, Poland ~+0.07, remainder ~+0.10 — roughly +0.85 pp above the advanced-economy core. Advanced core at 2.2% therefore prints ~3.0% on the literal construction. Resolver fork: I put 0.75 on the literal all-38 read (the question says OECD-38 explicitly; that is a strong textual signal, so I am above P3's 0.70), under which P = 0.90; 0.25 on a silent advanced-economy construction, under which P = 0.48 (above P1/P2 because care, trades and grid unit labour costs are the core of non-tradable services and my growth path is hotter). 0.75*0.90 + 0.25*0.48 = 0.795.\nConjunct B (China headline CPI <1.50%/yr, 2027-2036) = 0.50. I depart DOWNWARD from all three (0.62/0.62/0.72). Their Japan analogue is doing all the work, and Japan itself falsified it — a 25-year sub-1% regime broke to 2-3% in 2022-25, so the regime is demonstrably exitable inside a decade. Three specific pressures Wave 1 under-priced: (i) headline, not core, so the ~19% food weight means one 5% food year lifts a 10-year mean by ~0.35 pp; (ii) my China path is a reflating one (official p